# broiler-js reference datasets pin 2
#
# Broiler.VM's reference datasets of the time area, in two blocks, each the population of part C of an
# evidence bundle's rule:
#
# - Bundle sjl-8-001's rule (docs/evidence/sjl-8-001/decision-rule.md, committed at 15a1bd8f) takes
#   "Broiler.VM's src/tests/cldr/timezones/timezones.js and src/tests/temporal/temporal.js, byte for byte
#   as Broiler.VM's commit 9508bd5 holds them, with Broiler.VM's retained answer and divergences file for
#   each; SJL-8.4 copies them into this repository with their digests, and a copy whose digest differs is
#   not the population". The first block holds them, copied on 2026-10-17 (milestone SJL-8.4).
# - Bundle sjl-10-001's rule (docs/evidence/sjl-10-001/decision-rule.md, committed at 1d27064d) takes
#   "Broiler.VM's src/tests/temporal/temporal.js and src/tests/temporal/calendars/temporal-calendars.js,
#   byte for byte as Broiler.VM's commit 14072b0 holds them, each with Broiler.VM's retained polyfill
#   answer and divergences file", the first as the first block holds it, "copied from Broiler.VM's
#   9508bd5, whose three blobs are 14072b0's", and the second copied "at SJL-10.2 with its digests, and
#   datasets.pin gains its lines". The second block holds the second, copied on 2026-10-18 (milestone
#   SJL-10.2); the first block's lines are unchanged.
#
# WHERE THEY CAME FROM. Each file below was written by `git cat-file blob <source-commit>:<path>` in a
# Broiler.VM checkout, from the commit's object and not from a working tree, where <source-commit> is the
# one its block names. Its Git blob id is the one Broiler.VM's tree at that commit names for the path, so
# `git -C <Broiler.VM> rev-parse <source-commit>:<path>` prints it. Broiler.VM's 431c20e, the commit its
# own SJL-8 rule's bases were taken at, holds the first block's six blobs: `git diff --stat 9508bd5
# 431c20e -- src/tests/cldr/timezones src/tests/temporal` prints nothing. Broiler.VM's 14072b0 holds the
# three blobs of temporal.js's dataset that the first block pins, as the SJL-10 rule says, and its
# 99c9c9f, the commit that carries Broiler.VM's own SJL-10 rule, holds the same blobs of both programs:
# `git diff --stat 9508bd5 14072b0 -- src/tests/temporal/temporal.js
# src/tests/temporal/temporal.polyfill-e8cc03fc.txt src/tests/temporal/divergences.txt` and `git diff
# --stat 14072b0 99c9c9f -- src/tests/temporal` print nothing. The divergences files are renamed here,
# each after its program, because each is `divergences.txt` in Broiler.VM; nothing else is renamed, and
# no byte is changed.
#
# WHAT A LINE MEANS. `source-commit <commit>` names the Broiler.VM commit of the `file` lines after it, up
# to the next `source-commit` line; `copied` says when that block was copied. `file <name in this
# directory> <bytes> <sha256> <git blob id> <Broiler.VM path at the block's source-commit>`. `program`
# lines say which three files make one program's dataset: `program <name> <program> <retained answer>
# <divergences>`. The harness, scripts/conformance/run_reference_datasets.py, refuses to run when a
# file's length or SHA-256 differs from its line, and its tests hold every file to its line on every run
# of the conformance scripts' tests. To check by hand, in this directory:
#
#   awk '$1 == "file" { print $4 "  " $2 }' datasets.pin | sha256sum -c

source-repository Broiler.VM
